(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physiology-exam-4-bone-muscle-physiology-2-quiz"]={"title":"Smooth Muscle & Bone Physiology — Quiz (30 Questions)","category":"Physiology","questions":[{"q":"Unitary (visceral) smooth muscle, such as that of the gut and blood vessels, behaves as a functional syncytium mainly because:","choices":["Its cells are electrically coupled by gap junctions and contract in unison","Its cells are densely innervated and contract only when stimulated","Each cell is individually controlled by its own dedicated motor neuron","Its cells are physically fused into a single giant multinucleated fiber"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: unitary/visceral muscle is a sheet of electrically coupled cells that acts together, often spontaneously. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 40"},{"q":"Which structure is an example of MULTIUNITARY smooth muscle?","choices":["The muscular wall of the gut","The iris of the eye","The wall of a blood vessel","The muscular wall of the airways"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: the iris, along with the vas deferens and piloerectors, is listed as multiunitary. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 40"},{"q":"As defined in the lecture, a syncytium is:","choices":["A single cell containing one centrally located nucleus","A bundle of discrete cells each with its own separate innervation","A multinucleated mass of protoplasm produced by the merging of cells","A network of cells joined only by mechanical desmosome junctions"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: this is the lecture's exact definition of a syncytium. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 40"},{"q":"Compared with skeletal muscle, smooth muscle can shorten over a much larger range, achieving approximately what percentage of shortening?","choices":["10-20%","30-45%","85-95%","60-75%"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: smooth muscle can operate over a large length range, with 60-75% shortening possible. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 41"},{"q":"Smooth muscle is remarkably energy efficient; its oxygen consumption is approximately:","choices":["1% that of skeletal muscle at the same tension","10% that of skeletal muscle at the same tension","Equal to skeletal muscle at the same tension","Twice that of skeletal muscle at the same tension"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: the lecture states smooth muscle uses about 1% of the oxygen of an equal weight of skeletal muscle at the same tension. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 41"},{"q":"Which feature allows smooth muscle to maintain force for hours, days, or weeks with minimal energy expenditure?","choices":["Rapid cross-bridge cycling driven by high myosin ATPase activity","The latch state of attached cross-bridges","Continuous high-frequency firing of action potentials","Ongoing release of acetylcholine at a motor end plate"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: the latch state lets smooth muscle maintain force for prolonged periods. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 41"},{"q":"In smooth muscle, the structures that anchor thin filaments and are considered analogous to the Z discs of striated muscle are the:","choices":["Gap junctions","Intermediate filaments","Dense bodies","Terminal cisternae"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: dense bodies are the membrane-associated dense areas analogous to Z discs. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 41"},{"q":"How is neurotransmitter delivered at the smooth-muscle neuromuscular junction?","choices":["Through a single discrete motor end plate located on each fiber","From varicosities in diffuse junctions, diffusing to the muscle cells","By direct cytoplasmic transfer of transmitter through gap junctions","Via acetylcholinesterase packaged within the synaptic cleft"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: autonomic fibers form diffuse junctions whose varicosities release transmitter that diffuses to the cells. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 42"},{"q":"During smooth-muscle excitation-contraction coupling, calcium initiates contraction by binding to:","choices":["Troponin C on the thin filament","Calsequestrin within the sarcoplasmic reticulum","Calmodulin, since the troponin complex is absent","Tropomyosin, displacing it directly from actin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: with troponin absent, calcium binds calmodulin, which is structurally similar. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 43"},{"q":"Myosin light chain kinase (MLCK) can phosphorylate the regulatory light chain only when:","choices":["Calcium is bound to calmodulin, activating the kinase","Adenosine triphosphate levels fall below their resting baseline","Myosin light chain phosphatase is simultaneously active","Troponin C on the thin filament releases its bound calcium"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: MLCK is active only in the presence of calmodulin that has calcium bound. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 43"},{"q":"Relaxation of smooth muscle is produced when:","choices":["Myosin light chain kinase phosphorylates the regulatory light chain","Myosin light chain phosphatase dephosphorylates the light chain","Calcium binds to calmodulin to form an active complex","A second molecule of adenosine triphosphate is hydrolyzed"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: dephosphorylation of the myosin light chain by the phosphatase causes relaxation. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 44"},{"q":"In smooth-muscle cross-bridge cycling, what is the role of the SECOND molecule of adenosine triphosphate (ATP)?","choices":["It phosphorylates calmodulin to activate the kinase","It directly energizes myosin light chain phosphatase","It releases the myosin head from actin","It pumps calcium back into the sarcoplasmic reticulum"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: the first adenosine triphosphate supports phosphorylation, and a second is needed to release the head from actin. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 44"},{"q":"Which pairing of an ENDOCRINE agent with its effect on smooth muscle is correct?","choices":["Cholecystokinin causes gallbladder contraction","Nitric oxide causes gastric contraction","Histamine causes relaxation of blood vessels","Adenosine causes constriction of blood vessels"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: cholecystokinin is listed as an endocrine stimulus for gallbladder contraction. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 46"},{"q":"Which substance is a PARACRINE mediator that relaxes vascular smooth muscle?","choices":["Angiotensin II","Antidiuretic hormone","Nitric oxide","Cholecystokinin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: nitric oxide is a paracrine mediator that relaxes blood vessels. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 46"},{"q":"Collagen produced by osteoblasts requires which vitamin as a cofactor for the enzyme lysyl hydroxylase?","choices":["Vitamin K (phylloquinone)","Vitamin D (calciferol)","Vitamin C (ascorbic acid)","Vitamin A (retinol)"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: ascorbic acid is the required cofactor for lysyl hydroxylase in collagen synthesis. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 47"},{"q":"During ossification, osteoblasts secrete which set of products?","choices":["Type I collagen, bone sialoproteins, and osteocalcin","Cathepsin K, protons, and matrix metalloproteinases","Parathyroid hormone and calcitonin","Fully formed hydroxyapatite crystals ready for deposit"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: osteoblasts secrete mostly type I collagen along with bone sialoproteins and osteocalcin. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 47"},{"q":"Once the matrix calcifies around them, osteoblasts become which cell, residing within lacunae and canaliculi?","choices":["Osteoclasts","Chondroblasts","Fibroblasts","Osteocytes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: entombed osteoblasts become osteocytes within the canaliculi and lacunae. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 48"},{"q":"In the hardening of bone, what precipitates onto the collagen BEFORE calcium and phosphate salts are deposited?","choices":["Osteoprotegerin","Cathepsin K","Mucopolysaccharide organic glue","Hydroxyapatite crystals"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: hydroxyapatite crystals precipitate onto the collagen, after which calcium and phosphate salts are deposited. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 50"},{"q":"Type I collagen secreted by osteoblasts constitutes approximately what fraction of the protein in bone?","choices":["45%","70%","90%","100%"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: type I collagen makes up about 90% of the protein in bone. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 51"},{"q":"What is the function of osteocalcin, and which vitamin assists it?","choices":["It resorbs bone matrix; it is assisted by vitamin D","It bonds hydroxyapatite to collagen; it is assisted by vitamin K","It degrades collagen fibers; it is assisted by vitamin C","It transports calcium in the plasma; it is assisted by vitamin A"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: osteocalcin bonds hydroxyapatite to collagen, and vitamin K helps it do this. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 51"},{"q":"Which statement about osteocytes is correct?","choices":["They respond to mechanical (piezoelectric) stimuli and maintain the bone matrix","They are large multinucleated cells that actively resorb and remove mineralized bone","They secrete parathyroid hormone into the blood in response to low serum calcium","They are immature dividing cells found only within growth plates"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: osteocytes sense mechanical/piezoelectric stimuli and are responsible for maintaining the matrix. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 53"},{"q":"Osteoclasts, the large multinucleated bone-resorbing cells, are derived from the fusion of which progenitor cells?","choices":["Osteoprogenitor (mesenchymal) cells","Chondrocytes of the epiphyseal growth plate","Parafollicular C-cells of the thyroid gland","Granulocyte/macrophage progenitor cells"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: osteoclasts arise from fusion of granulocyte/macrophage progenitor (hemopoietic) cells. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 55"},{"q":"The shallow resorption bay formed in bone directly beneath an active osteoclast is called:","choices":["A canaliculus","Howship's lacuna","The epiphyseal plate","The ruffled border"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: the resorption bay under an osteoclast is known as Howship's lacuna. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 55"},{"q":"Osteoclasts degrade bone matrix by releasing protons together with which enzymes across the ruffled border?","choices":["Alkaline phosphatase and osteonectin","Lysyl hydroxylase and osteocalcin","Myosin light chain kinase and calmodulin","Cathepsin K and matrix metalloproteinases"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: osteoclasts release protons plus cathepsin K and matrix metalloproteinases to degrade the matrix. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 57"},{"q":"Which pair correctly matches each hormone with its effect on blood calcium?","choices":["Parathyroid hormone lowers calcium; calcitonin raises calcium","Both parathyroid hormone and calcitonin raise blood calcium","Parathyroid hormone raises calcium; calcitonin lowers calcium","Both parathyroid hormone and calcitonin lower blood calcium"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: parathyroid hormone raises blood calcium while calcitonin lowers it. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 59"},{"q":"Chondrocytes in the epiphyseal growth plates are primarily regulated by:","choices":["Calcitonin secreted by the parafollicular C-cells of the thyroid","Insulin-like growth factor I (IGF-I) produced by the liver","Parathyroid hormone from the parathyroid glands","Cathepsin K released by activated osteoclasts"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: growth-plate chondrocytes are regulated by IGF-I, made by the liver in response to growth hormone. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 59"},{"q":"Which statement about parathyroid hormone (PTH) and plasma calcium is correct?","choices":["PTH is released when calcium-sensing receptors detect low serum calcium","Roughly 60% of plasma calcium is protein bound and biologically inactive","Protein-bound calcium is the only biologically active form","PTH is released whenever serum calcium rises above the normal reference range"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: calcium-sensing receptors in the parathyroid gland trigger PTH release when serum calcium falls. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 60"},{"q":"How does parathyroid hormone (PTH) alter renal handling of calcium and phosphate?","choices":["It increases reabsorption of both calcium and phosphate","It increases phosphate reabsorption in the proximal tubule and increases calcium excretion","It decreases calcium reabsorption and decreases phosphate excretion","It increases calcium reabsorption in the distal tubule and increases phosphate excretion"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: PTH raises distal-tubule calcium reabsorption and inhibits proximal phosphate reabsorption, increasing phosphate excretion. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 61"},{"q":"By what mechanism does parathyroid hormone (PTH) increase osteoclast formation?","choices":["By binding receptors directly on mature osteoclasts to activate their resorption","By stimulating osteoblasts to produce RANKL and decreasing osteoprotegerin (OPG)","By increasing osteoprotegerin (OPG) so that RANKL is released","By converting growth-plate chondrocytes into mature osteoclasts"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: PTH makes osteoblasts form RANKL and release M-CSF while lowering OPG, promoting osteoclast differentiation. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 63"},{"q":"Calcitonin, secreted by thyroid C-cells during hypercalcemia, lowers blood calcium primarily by:","choices":["Directly stimulating osteoclasts to accelerate bone resorption","Increasing intestinal absorption of dietary calcium","Activating myosin light chain kinase within bone cells","Inhibiting osteoclasts and thereby reducing bone resorption"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: calcitonin acts mainly by inhibiting osteoclasts, reducing bone resorption and lowering blood calcium. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 64"}]};
